---
import Card from "./Card.astro";
import Label from "./Label.astro";
---

<section id="safety" class="border-b border-neutral-950 py-20 sm:py-28">
	<Label tone="alert">02 / Paranoid safety</Label>
	<h2
		class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
	>
		Two rules uppy enforces no matter what your config says.
	</h2>
	<p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
		Renovate makes these optional. uppy makes them non-negotiable — if your
		config asks for less, uppy ignores it.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
		<Card
			kicker="Minimum release age"
			title="A 3-day floor, always"
			tone="alert"
		>
			<p>
				uppy will not recommend a version until it has been published for at
				least three days. Set a shorter cooldown in config and it makes no
				difference — the paranoid floor wins. Time in the wild is the cheapest
				safety check there is, so uppy never skips it.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card
			kicker="Digest pinning"
			title="The sha is the ground truth"
			tone="alert"
		>
			<p>
				Every unpinned GitHub Action gets pinned to the exact commit sha its tag
				resolves to —
				<code class="font-mono text-neutral-950">@&lt;sha&gt; # v4.1.0</code>.
				The trailing comment is attacker-controllable, so uppy never trusts it:
				it reverse-resolves the sha to a tag, because the sha is what GitHub
				actually checks out.
			</p>
		</Card>
	</div>

	<!-- vulnerability scanning sub-block: one shipped, two upcoming -->
	<h3 class="mt-20 max-w-3xl text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
		And it watches for known vulnerabilities.
	</h3>
	<p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
		Age and pinning guard against the unknown. Advisories cover the known —
		surfaced right on the Dependency Dashboard, with more on the way.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
		<Card kicker="Available now" title="OSV + GitHub alerts">
			<p>
				uppy queries the OSV database for your npm dependencies and reads GitHub
				and Dependabot security advisories, flagging every affected package
				directly on the Dependency Dashboard.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card kicker="Advisories" title="Snyk & more sources" upcoming>
			<p>
				More advisory feeds — Snyk and others — layered on top of OSV and
				GitHub, so a vulnerability flagged anywhere gets caught here.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card kicker="AI threat scan" title="Reads the source diff" upcoming>
			<p>
				AI reads the actual code changes between your installed version and the
				proposed one, flagging suspicious additions — new network calls,
				obfuscation, install scripts — before the update reaches you.
			</p>
		</Card>
	</div>
</section>
